  data-description='Graphic design case study for Heart Asylum, focused on a flexible logo system and atmospheric cover artwork for music and social platforms.'
  data-case='heartasylum'
  aria-labelledby='case-heartasylum-logotype-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Graphic Design</p>
        <h1 id='case-heartasylum-logotype-title'>Heart Asylum</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>
          A dark logo suite and atmospheric release artwork created for an alternative rock duo.
        </p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Logo design and cover artwork</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Real-world music branding</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Deliverables</dt>
            <dd>Logo suite and single cover</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>Adobe Illustrator, Photoshop</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Release-ready visual identity</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='heartasylum-logotype'></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        Heart Asylum needed a recognisable identity that matched the dark, alternative character of
        the duo's music. It had to work across several formats, including streaming platforms,
        social graphics, posters, and release artwork. The single cover also needed to feel
        connected to the identity while having its own composition and atmosphere.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            Music branding needs to communicate a mood quickly, but many people first see it as a
            small image on a streaming or social platform. The main challenge was creating something
            expressive and detailed that would still have a clear silhouette and remain recognisable
            when reduced in size.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Balance a dramatic visual style with clarity at smaller sizes
            </li>
            <li>
              Create a system that could work as an icon, wordmark, or complete logo
            </li>
            <li>
              Connect the logo and cover artwork without using the same composition twice
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/logo-heartasylum-logosuite-600.webp'
            data-src='logo-heartasylum-logosuite-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='389'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Heart Asylum logo suite showing the primary lockup, heart icon, wordmark, secondary lockup, and reversed version'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Primary, secondary, icon, wordmark, and reversed logo variations
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              I built the emblem around a heart with sharp, architectural linework. This connects
              the emotional meaning of the name with the harder edge of the music.
            </li>
            <li>
              I created primary, secondary, icon, wordmark, and reversed versions so the identity
              could adapt to different backgrounds and formats.
            </li>
            <li>
              For the cover, I used high-contrast wing linework as the main visual element, with
              restrained typography and portrait detail supporting the atmosphere.
            </li>
            <li>
              I checked the square artwork at a smaller size to make sure the wings, title, and band
              mark kept a clear visual hierarchy.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/cover-heartasylum-used-to-believe-600.webp'
            data-src='cover-heartasylum-used-to-believe-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='600'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Heart Asylum single cover for Used to Believe in Angels, with white wing linework over a dark portrait'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>
            Used to Believe in Angels — square cover artwork designed for digital release
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The result is a connected set of assets rather than only one logo. The different logo
        versions provide practical options for small digital placements and larger graphics, while
        the cover carries the same dark character in a more atmospheric way. This project helped me
        develop a visual system that communicates the feeling of the music while still working
        across very different sizes and formats.
      </p>
    </div>

